    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "The Court overrules Williams v. Florida, 399 U.S. 78 (1970), holding that under the original public meaning of the Sixth Amendment as incorporated by the Fourteenth Amendment and following Ramos v. Louisiana, 590 U.S. 83 (2020), the right to trial by an impartial jury requires a twelve-person jury in serious criminal prosecutions."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The Court announces a categorical constitutional rule requiring twelve-person juries at empanelment for serious felony prosecutions in federal and state court, while leaving undisturbed petty-offense trials, post-empanelment good-cause excusals under Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 23(b)(3), and civil juries under the Seventh Amendment."
    }
  ],
